The Linux boot process means:

What happens inside a computer from the moment you press the power button until Linux is ready to use.

=> Think of it like starting a car:

BIOS/UEFI → checks the car
GRUB → decides which route/system to start
Kernel → starts controlling everything
initramfs → helps find the main filesystem
systemd → starts all required services
Login → system is ready for the user

=> The complete flow

Power ON
   ↓
BIOS / UEFI
   ↓
GRUB Bootloader
   ↓
Linux Kernel
   ↓
initramfs
   ↓
systemd (PID 1)
   ↓
Services
   ↓
Login Screen / SSH

=> Let's understand each step.

What is Firmware?

Firmware is software that is built into a hardware device and helps that hardware work.

In very simple words:

Firmware is the small software that tells hardware how to start and perform its basic functions.

a) When you press the Power button, Linux is not running yet.

First, the computer's firmware starts.

On a modern PC, this firmware is usually:

BIOS — older technology
UEFI — newer technology

The firmware performs some initial hardware checks and then looks for something that can boot an operating 
system which is called as bootloader

c) GRUB stands for:

GRand Unified Bootloader

Its main job is to find and load the Linux kernel.

GRUB-related files are commonly found under:

/boot/grub/

You can check:

ls /boot/grub/

d) The kernel is the core of the Linux operating system.

Think of the kernel as the middleman between software and hardware.

For example:

Applications
     ↓
Linux Kernel
     ↓
Hardware

The kernel manages things like:

CPU
RAM
Disk
Network
Processes
Hardware devices
Drivers

=> Check your kernel

Run:

uname -r

Example:

6.8.0-31-generic

This tells you the currently running kernel version.

You can also use:

uname -a

e) initramfs means:

initial RAM filesystem

It is a small temporary filesystem loaded into RAM during the early boot process.

Why do we need it?

Suppose your real / filesystem is on a disk.

The kernel needs some tools/drivers before it can access the real root filesystem.

=> You may see files like:

/boot/initrd.img-6.8.0-31-generic

You can check:

ls -lh /boot/initrd*

f) the system starts its init system.

On modern Ubuntu and RHEL systems, this is usually:

systemd

And very importantly:

systemd = PID 1

=> What is PID?

PID means:

Process ID

Every running process gets a process ID.

=> Check it:

ps -p 1

You may see:

PID TTY          TIME CMD
1   ?        00:00:02 systemd

Or:

ps -p 1 -o pid,comm

Output:

PID COMMAND
1   systemd

g) Now systemd starts the services that Linux needs.

=> You can check a service using:

Ubuntu commonly:

systemctl status ssh

On some distributions, the service is named:

systemctl status sshd

For example, you can check:

systemctl status ssh

If it is running, you may see:

Active: active (running)

h) Now the system becomes ready for users.

i) On a desktop Ubuntu machine:

Boot
 ↓
Services
 ↓
Login Screen
 ↓
Username + Password
 ↓
Desktop

ii) On a Linux server, there may not be a graphical login screen.

Instead:

Boot
 ↓
Services
 ↓
SSH starts
 ↓
Server is ready
 ↓
Administrator connects using SSH

For example:

ssh user@server-ip

Note:- Put Everything Together

Now let's connect all the pieces:

                 POWER ON
                    ↓
              BIOS / UEFI
                    ↓
        Check hardware + boot device
                    ↓
                  GRUB
                    ↓
          Find and load Kernel
                    ↓
              Linux Kernel
                    ↓
               initramfs
                    ↓
       Find/mount real root filesystem
                    ↓
                systemd
                 PID 1
                    ↓
          Start system services
                    ↓
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          ↓                   ↓
      GUI Login            SSH Login
          ↓                   ↓
       Desktop             Server Ready
